WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.302
Injection prevention should be visible in design, code review, and tests.

00:00:04.302 --> 00:00:07.520
Reviewers should ask where untrusted data enters, where it flows,

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.640
and which sensitive sinks it reaches.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:13.960
Sensitive sinks include database queries, shell commands, templates, browser rendering,

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:17.260
file paths, headers, URLs, parsers, and downstream APIs.

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:19.055
If data reaches one of those locations,

00:00:19.055 --> 00:00:22.335
the review should identify the context-specific control.

00:00:22.335 --> 00:00:25.295
Security-focused tests should cover schemas, negative cases, boundaries,

00:00:25.295 --> 00:00:26.727
and high-risk transformations.

00:00:26.727 --> 00:00:29.160
A test can confirm that unknown fields are rejected,

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:33.785
that unsupported operators are not accepted, that route parameters are treated as values,

00:00:33.785 --> 00:00:35.595
that rendering paths use safe APIs,

00:00:35.595 --> 00:00:39.200
and that command execution paths only allow approved operations.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.640
These tests should use safe representative inputs rather than exploit payload collections.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:47.570
Static analysis and dependency review can help, but they are not complete by themselves.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:52.560
Tools may find string-built queries, risky process execution calls, raw HTML rendering,

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.202
or unsafe evaluation patterns.

00:00:54.202 --> 00:00:58.395
Human review is still needed to understand data flow, framework behavior, business intent,

00:00:58.395 --> 00:00:59.775
and compensating controls.

00:00:59.775 --> 00:01:01.890
Least privilege and defense in depth reduce impact.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:05.780
Database accounts should have only the permissions the application needs.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:07.520
Processes should run with constrained privileges.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.880
Logging and monitoring should identify suspicious validation failures without exposing

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:12.500
secrets.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:16.080
Content Security Policy, safe defaults, and framework protections can add layers,

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.927
but they should not be treated as permission to build unsafe sinks.

00:01:19.927 --> 00:01:21.562
Document assumptions and sensitive sinks.

00:01:21.562 --> 00:01:24.008
Future developers need to know why a field is allowlisted,

00:01:24.008 --> 00:01:28.370
why a query builder escape hatch is prohibited, why a template path rejects user content,

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:30.775
or why a rendering helper is required.

00:01:30.775 --> 00:01:33.600
Secure design becomes easier to maintain when the reasoning is visible.
